          "contents": [
            {
              "name": "§4.1 'Faraday's Law of Induction': the emf as ∮E·dr, the flux rule E = -dΦ/dt, and Lenz's law",
              "disposition": "inline",
              "item": "def-em-moving-circuit-geometry",
              "support": "The emf line-integral reading and its orientation and sign conventions are absorbed into the definition of the moving material circuit; the fixed-patch Faraday identity itself is supplied by pthm-em-integral-maxwell (batch 24)."
            },
            {
              "name": "§4.1.1 'Faraday's Law for Moving Wires': the sliding bar E = vBd; the general moving loop C(t) with velocity v and the swept-surface derivation of dΦ/dt = -∮(E+v×B)·dr",
              "disposition": "included",
              "item": "pthm-em-motional-emf",
              "support": "The moving-loop transport formula and its conclusion emf = -Φ' are exactly the claimed content, with the C2/no-singularity-crossing hypotheses and the frozen-patch Faraday step made explicit in the scaffold."
            },
            {
              "name": "§4.1.1, sliding-bar paragraph: current opposes the motion; charges moving in the bar feel the Lorentz force and the bar is slowed",
              "disposition": "included",
              "item": "texp-em-sliding-bar-emf",
              "support": "The instantaneous motional emf and the Lorentz force on the bar are built as the idealised thought experiment; its power-balance clause replaces the qualitative 'bar is slowed' statement, whose full coupled dynamics needs a mechanical model and is not built."
            },
            {
              "name": "§4.1.2 'Inductance and Magnetostatic Energy': the inductance L = Φ/I, its dependence only on the circuit geometry, and the solenoid evaluation L = μ0N²V",
              "disposition": "included",
              "item": "pthm-em-inductance-reciprocity",
              "support": "The flux-linkage definition is realised for the declared smooth compact conserved current profiles and made reciprocal and finite; the ideal thin-solenoid evaluation itself is a declared ideal model and is not assigned a value in this pair."
            },
            {
              "name": "§4.1.2, magnetostatic energy: work against the induced emf, W = ½LI² = ½IΦ, rewritten as ½∫d³x J·A and then (1/2μ0)∫d³x B·B under a decay assumption",
              "disposition": "included",
              "item": "pthm-em-inductor-energy",
              "support": "The terminal-energy bookkeeping and its identification with the frozen magnetic field energy are proved for the lumped model and its smooth-profile realisation; the decay assumption is replaced by the explicit compact-support energy identity of lem-em-newtonian-curl-field-energy."
            },
            {
              "name": "§4.1.3 'Resistance': Ohm's law E = IR as an extra, material-dependent input; V = IR",
              "disposition": "included",
              "item": "post-em-circuit-approximation",
              "support": "The resistive branch relation is adopted as part of the lumped circuit postulate, with the Ohmic material branch of post-em-material-closure (batch 29) and the source-scope caveat recorded."
            },
            {
              "name": "§4.1.3, sliding-bar dynamics with resistance: mẍ = IBd with I = E/R and the exponential decay ẋ(t) = -v exp(-B²d²t/mR); Joule heating I²R",
              "disposition": "out-of-scope",
              "reason": "The coupled bar-plus-circuit motion uses a Newtonian mechanical mass model and solves a joint mechanical-electrical ODE; this pair adopts a prescribed-motion idealisation and proves only the instantaneous emf and power balance, so the dynamics and the Joule-heating energetics are not built here."
            },
            {
              "name": "§4.1.4 'Michael Faraday (1791-1867)': biography and historical context",
              "disposition": "out-of-scope",
              "reason": "A biographical and historical narrative carrying no mathematical or physical result claimed by this pair; the historical experimental record belongs to the framework's empirical-test page and is not a proof ingredient."
            }
