          "contents": [
            {
              "name": "§4.2 'Faraday's law': the e.m.f. around a closed loop equals minus the rate of change of the magnetic flux; ∇×E = -∂B/∂t via Stokes; flux well-definedness requires ∇·B = 0",
              "disposition": "inline",
              "item": "pthm-em-motional-emf",
              "support": "The flux-rule statement and the field-side identity are the two inputs combined in the moving-loop theorem; the book's fixed-loop statement is the frozen-patch case of pthm-em-integral-maxwell."
            },
            {
              "name": "§7.1 'Introduction': scope of the chapter on magnetic induction",
              "disposition": "out-of-scope",
              "reason": "A chapter-scope declaration, not a mathematical or physical result; the pair records its own scope and model assumptions explicitly in the scaffolded items."
            },
            {
              "name": "§7.2 'Inductance': two stationary loops, the flux Φ₂ = M I₁ proportional to the current by Biot-Savart linearity",
              "disposition": "included",
              "item": "pthm-em-inductance-reciprocity",
              "support": "The linear linkage is proved for smooth compact conserved profiles via the double-integral coefficient and the Fubini representation; the book's thin-loop reading is treated as the declared ideal model."
            },
            {
              "name": "§7.3 'Self-inductance': the long-solenoid self-inductance L = μ0 N² π r² l",
              "disposition": "out-of-scope",
              "reason": "The thin-solenoid self-inductance is an idealised noncompact thin-wire value; this pair deliberately assigns finite self-coefficients only to smooth compact profiles and states that zero-radius wire self-inductance is not assigned a value."
            },
            {
              "name": "§7.4 'Mutual inductance': the superimposed-solenoid mutual inductance M = μ0N₁N₂πr²l, M = √(L₁L₂) under perfect linkage, and the leakage warning (M somewhat less in reality)",
              "disposition": "included",
              "item": "ex-em-transformer-idealization",
              "support": "The perfect-linkage bound and the ideal common-flux relations are built as the worked example, with the leakage and loss limitations stated."
            },
            {
              "name": "§7.5 'Magnetic energy': the circuit equation V = L dI/dt + RI for a coil; the two-coil battery work ∫(V₁I₁+V₂I₂)dt = Ohmic terms + ½L₁I₁² + ½L₂I₂² + MI₁I₂; the bound M ≤ √(L₁L₂); the statement that the energy stored in an inductor is stored in the magnetic field",
              "disposition": "included",
              "item": "pthm-em-inductor-energy",
              "support": "The power bookkeeping and its field-energy identification are proved; the two-coil energy and the mutual-inductance bound are instantiated in the companion transformer example."
            },
            {
              "name": "§7.5, RL circuit equation V = L dI/dt + RI with battery source",
              "disposition": "included",
              "item": "pthm-em-rl-lc-dynamics",
              "support": "The scalar RL initial value problem, its unique solution and the power split are proved for continuous source voltages, with the constant-voltage transient on the B page."
            },
            {
              "name": "§7.6 'Alternating current circuits': the ideal element relations V = IR, V = L dI/dt, V = ∫I dt/C; the LCR circuit equation; impedance and resonance at ω = 1/√(LC)",
              "disposition": "included",
              "item": "post-em-circuit-approximation",
              "support": "The ideal element relations, the loop balance and the smallness/idealisation qualifications are adopted in the circuit postulate; the lossless LC oscillation is proved as a conditional model computation rather than through the book's steady-state phasor route."
            },
            {
              "name": "§7.6, resonant LCR circuit: current amplitude |V₀|/√((ωL-1/ωC)²+R²) peaking at ω = 1/√(LC)",
              "disposition": "out-of-scope",
              "reason": "The driven steady-state phasor resonance is a different regime from the lossless LC initial value problem and the RL transient built here; its steady-state periodic analysis is not promised by this pair's inventory."
            },
            {
              "name": "§7.7 'Transmission lines': the distributed inductance/capacitance telegrapher model and the breakdown of the lumped description at high frequency",
              "disposition": "out-of-scope",
              "reason": "Distributed transmission-line theory defines where the lumped reduction fails; the pair adopts the lumped model explicitly and does not develop the telegrapher equation or characteristic impedance content."
            }
